"role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_20", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "1006 never contacts 1860 directly (step_06). A static directed path exists via hubs: 1006->1680->153->1860 (steps 17, 18, 11) and 1006->45->153->1860 (steps 13, 19, 11). However, every tool in this session aggregates over the full horizon [0,100000] and returns no per-event timestamps, so I cannot verify that these hops occur inside [18031,56431], nor that they are strictly increasing in time with gaps of at most 600 units \u2014 the two properties that define the claimed cascade. Confounder checks argue against accepting the path on static grounds alone: the intermediaries 153 (334 events), 1680 (170) and 45 (141) are high-degree hubs (steps 07, 08) in a sparse, near-non-repeating graph (repeat_ratio 0.034, step_01), so static connectivity between almost any pair is close to guaranteed and carries little evidence about timing. Meanwhile the endpoints themselves are low-activity (9 and 11 events, step_02), so volume/popularity does not support a cascade either. Each hop is backed by a single event, giving no timing redundancy, and the window spans only ~38% of the horizon (step_20) while the 600-unit gap budget is ~0.6% of it \u2014 so the temporal constraints are far from automatically satisfied. Verdict: insufficient evidence; a timestamp-level or window-restricted query would be needed to decide."}, "raw_final_response": "The window covers only ~38% of the horizon (step_20), and the required inter-hop gap (600) is 0.6% of the horizon \u2014 a very tight constraint that a purely static path is unlikely to satisfy by chance. Without timestamps I cannot confirm or refute it.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_636b848d\",\n  \"decision\": \"insufficient\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1006, 1860, 45, 153, 1680],\n  \"window\": [18031, 56431],\n  \"confidence\": 0.7,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_17\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_18\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_19\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_20\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"1006 never contacts 1860 directly (step_06). A static directed path exists via hubs: 1006->1680->153->1860 (steps 17, 18, 11) and 1006->45->153->1860 (steps 13, 19, 11). However, every tool in this session aggregates over the full horizon [0,100000] and returns no per-event timestamps, so I cannot verify that these hops occur inside [18031,56431], nor that they are strictly increasing in time with gaps of at most 600 units \u2014 the two properties that define the claimed cascade. Confounder checks argue against accepting the path on static grounds alone: the intermediaries 153 (334 events), 1680 (170) and 45 (141) are high-degree hubs (steps 07, 08) in a sparse, near-non-repeating graph (repeat_ratio 0.034, step_01), so static connectivity between almost any pair is close to guaranteed and carries little evidence about timing. Meanwhile the endpoints themselves are low-activity (9 and 11 events, step_02), so volume/popularity does not support a cascade either. Each hop is backed by a single event, giving no timing redundancy, and the window spans only ~38% of the horizon (step_20) while the 600-unit gap budget is ~0.6% of it \u2014 so the temporal constraints are far from automatically satisfied. Verdict: insufficient evidence; a timestamp-level or window-restricted query would be needed to decide.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 52685, "total_output_tokens": 7049, "total_tokens": 59734, "total_latency_ms": 102574, "llm_calls": 9, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}